we begin in afghanistan where a taliban  spokesman says the group will not extend  the deadline for the withdrawal of  western forces the armed group has  warned of consequences if u.s and allied  forces do not withdraw by the august  31st deadline thousands of troops are  deployed in kabul to evacuate their  citizens and vulnerable afghans there  have been chaotic and desperate scenes  at the airport over the last week and at  least 20 people have been killed the u.s  president says the pace of evacuations  is accelerating he hopes not to extend  the deadline but he hasn't ruled it out  and as attention focuses on the  evacuation efforts the world health  organization is warning of a wider  humanitarian crisis it says 500 tons of  critical medical supplies are stuck in  the uae because of the holdup at kabul  airports meanwhile on the ground the  taliban says it has sent fighters to  panshire to counter resistance fighters  let's get more now from charlotte bellas  she begins our coverage with this  reports on the situation at kabul  airport  we're at the north gate of kabul  international airport this is where most  people ask to come to and in recent days  it has been closed because of what the  us says is security threats from isil  but for most people here the biggest  threat is dehydration is a stampede and  as constant gunfire to actually get to  this point they have to run the gauntlet  first we moved through taliban special  forces they're known as the badri 313  and they control the perimeter of the  airport if you can get past them and  convince them that you deserve to be  able to get to this point and get on a  flight out on one of these evacuation  flights then you enter a zone which is  held by afghan forces some of the last  afghan forces in the country they are  called the 01s and the o2s they are  funded by the cia and they have come to  this area as a bridge between the  taliban and the american they are also  doing checks and holding security and  essentially what is a no-man's land once  you get past them you end up where we  are now which has no security forces  around and is just  thousands of people gathering waiting  and being overlooked by western security  forces who essentially hold the key to  the gate and will decide whether these  people leave the country or do not  well we have two correspondents  reporting on the tension over a possible  extension of the deadline to withdraw  nato forces nadine baba will have the  uk's response on that from london first  though let's head to rob mcbride he's in  the afghan capital for us rob the  taliban then saying there will be no  extension to that deadline is august  31st then they want the foreign troops  out  that's right and warning of consequences  but it really remains to be seen what  consequences are they talking about and  at this stage what action would they be  prepared to take i mean you you have to  ask you know whose deadline is this  anyway it does seem to be something of a  movable feast originally the deadline  was set at may the first for the  withdrawal of u.s troops and nato forces  that was under the agreement reached  between the trump administration with  the taliban last year then in april of  this year president joe biden pushed it  back famously to the symbolic date of  september the 11th then last month he  brought it forward again to august  august the 31st so this august the 31st  date was set by biden in theory he can  now unset it the taliban doesn't like it  they say this is breaks with the  agreement that they signed last year but  the americans say well the taliban has  already broken that agreement in in any  case uh and it's difficult to see  what the taliban would do if it's done  just a few days past uh august the 31st  because as far as the americans are  concerned they can probably still get  everyone out before september the 11th  and i think at this point that the  taliban has a lot of other concerns more  pressing concerns as it tries to take  over and run a country rather than  continuing to have a scrap with the  americans so it'll talk tough i think  it'll talk about consequences i don't  really know what those consequences are  likely to be they may well turn out to  be very little meanwhile at the airport  itself we know that uh that chaos has  continued i think everyone wants to see  an end to that it's in everybody's  benefit  the us especially to see this nightmare  over but also as far as the taliban are  concerned they want an end to that and  in the meantime we do have now this  announcement from the world health  organization that  the  we could be using the arrival and  departure of these aircrafts more  intelligently because of course they  take various they full up fill up with  civilians or people from different  nationalities out of kabul but largely  arrive empty so why not put emergency  relief supplies on those planes for what  is a growing humanitarian  needs here in afghanistan in particular  this 500 tons  of medical supplies that the who says is  sitting in dubai which empty planes  could be bringing here into kabul  okay rob mcbride there bringing us up to  date from cardboard rob thank you  well the uk defense secretary ben  wallace says british troops will leave  afghanistan alongside u.s forces he says  it's unrealistic for them to stay any  longer g7 leaders are meeting on tuesday  to discuss coordination on the  evacuation process nadine baba is live  for us in london the demon the uk then  saying it will leave alongside the  americans  does that mean they're aiming for an  august 31st pull out too  well not quite hala what they're saying  is that they can't do things on their  own but they're really hoping that the  americans will actually give them uh at  least a few days more to get people out  of afghanistan on monday the uk's armed  forces minister uh james hippie has said  that the uk's got more than 6 000  individuals out of afghanistan in 